# mini-baas — DevOps & CI/CD Foundation (Phase 0)

> **The DevOps Shield: Shift-Left Quality Control**
> A robust, container-native pipeline designed to catch errors before they enter the repository, ensuring the `mini-baas` engine remains stable, strictly typed, and independently verifiable across all development environments.

---

## 1. Executive Summary

As `mini-baas` evolves into a complex Polyglot App Factory, the cost of debugging runtime errors multiplies. Phase 0 of the DevOps strategy focuses on the **"Shift-Left"** principle: catching formatting, linting, and typing errors on the developer's local machine exactly at the moment of the `git commit`, rather than waiting for the cloud CI to fail.

This document outlines the foundational DevOps architecture implemented to protect the `main` and `develop` branches without adding friction or requiring host-level software dependencies.

---

## 2. The "Only Docker" Philosophy

A core requirement for `mini-baas` (and subsequently `prismática`) is that developers only need `Docker` and `Make` installed on their host machines. 

### Why we rejected standard Node.js hook managers
Industry-standard tools like `Husky` or `Lefthook` require Node.js or specific binaries to be installed on the host operating system to intercept Git hooks. In restrictive environments (like 42 iMacs) or heterogeneous teams (Linux, macOS, Windows), this breaks the "Fully Containerized" promise.

**The Solution:** We implemented a purely native, Bash-based pre-commit hook generated directly by our `Makefile`. It acts as a bridge, intercepting the host's Git action and delegating the execution to the Docker container.

---

## 3. The DevOps Shield (Local Git Hooks)

The `install-hooks` command establishes our local wall. When a developer attempts to commit code, the shield evaluates the state of the container infrastructure.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    DEV[Developer types 'git commit'] --> HOOK{Is baas-dev-engine UP?}

    HOOK -->|No| WARN[Show Warning]
    WARN --> PROMPT{Force commit?}
    PROMPT -->|Yes| COMMIT_OK[Commit Created]
    PROMPT -->|No| ABORT[Commit Aborted]

    HOOK -->|Yes| LINT[make lint]
    LINT --> TYPE[make typecheck]
    TYPE --> CHECK{Exit Code == 0?}

    CHECK -->|No| ABORT_ERR[Commit Blocked\nFix your code!]
    CHECK -->|Yes| COMMIT_SEC[Commit Created]

    COMMIT_OK --> PUSH[git push]
    COMMIT_SEC --> PUSH

    PUSH --> CI[GitHub Actions CI\nUltimate Judge]

    style HOOK fill:#3498db,color:#fff
    style ABORT_ERR fill:#e74c3c,color:#fff
    style COMMIT_SEC fill:#2ecc71,color:#000
    style CI fill:#9b59b6,color:#fff
```

### Makefile Integration
The shield is automatically deployed during the project setup via the `make bootstrap` command, making the security layer completely invisible and automatic for new team members.

